    "final_answer": "I have obtained both values from WolframAlpha. Here are the results:\n\n## Electrical Resistivity at 20°C\n\n| Material | Electrical Resistivity (Ω·cm) | Assumed Condition |\n|----------|------------------------------|-------------------|\n| **UNS A92024** (Aluminum 2024 alloy) | **4.865 × 10⁻⁶ Ω·cm** | Annealed, O temper |\n| **UNS G10800** (Carbon steel, AISI 1080) | **1.8 × 10⁻⁵ Ω·cm** | Oil quenched at 815°C, tempered at 480°C |\n\n### Notes:\n- WolframAlpha displayed an initial result that appeared to multiply the resistivity by 20°C (showing units of \"cm · °C · Ω\"). When I clicked \"Show details\", it revealed the actual resistivity values in Ω·cm (欧·厘米), which represent the volume resistivity (体积电阻率) at 20°C.\n- These values are consistent with published reference data: aluminum 2024 has a volume resistivity of about 3.4-5.0 × 10⁻⁶ Ω·cm depending on temper, and medium-carbon steel (1080) has a volume resistivity of approximately 1.7-2.0 × 10⁻⁵ Ω·cm depending on heat treatment.",
